<section id="philosophy">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-label">Engineering the Encounter</div>
    <h2>Why I don't "sell." I build systems.</h2>

    <div class="purple-squirrel-box">
      <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--white-warm); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.7;">
        For 15 years, I treated every pedagogical encounter as a fragile, singular event. 
        When a system relies on human judgment to not break, it is not a product—it is a dependency. 
        I used to confuse <strong>the integrity of my craft</strong> with <strong>the vulnerability of sales</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="adr-timeline">
        <div class="adr-entry">
          <div class="adr-title">The Architectural Pivot</div>
          <p class="adr-desc">
            I stopped asking students to "buy me." Instead, I built <strong>huhuGERMAN</strong>. 
            By codifying pedagogical domain knowledge into Zod schemas and ADRs, I created a 
            <strong>Separation of Concerns</strong>. 
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="adr-entry">
          <div class="adr-title">The Result</div>
          <p class="adr-desc">
            The platform acts as the filter. It serves only those with the disposition 
            for the rigor I design for. When the encounter happens, it is not a 
            transactional negotiation—it is a recognition of shared purpose.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <div class="intersection-note">
        I don't look for students to convince. I look for the 1% who recognize the system I've built.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


